\text{circle area } = \text{pie area}
\end{align*}

Let $\theta$ be the central angle subtended by the diameter with endpoints $A(2,5)$ and $B(10,5)$. Since they both have the same $y$-coordinate, $\theta$ is twice the angle between the $y$-axis and the line segment $AB$.

Let $AB$ have length $d$ and use the distance formula to find it:
\begin{align*}
d = \sqrt{(10-2)^2 + (5-5)^2} = \sqrt{81} = 9
\end{align*}

Now we want to use $\theta$ to construct a unit circle around point $A$.
Since the diameter passes through the center of the circle, $A$ lies on the circumference of the circle. Thus, the radius of the circle $r = d/2 = 4.5$.

Using the definition of $\pi$, we know that if we have a circle with circumference $C$ and diameter $d$,
\begin{align*}
\frac{C}{2 \pi r} = 1
\end{align*}
Plugging in our calculated values, we have
\begin{align*}
\frac{(2d)}{2 \pi r} = 1 \\
\therefore \ C = \frac{4 \pi r}{1} = 4 \pi r
\end{align*}
Also, the sector area formula becomes
\begin{align*}
\text{sector area } = \frac{\theta}{2 \pi} \text{ pie area }= \frac{\theta}{2 \pi} C
\end{align*}
Now we can find the circle/pie area:
\begin{align*}
\boxed{\text{ pie area } = \frac{C}{2} = 2 \pi r}
\end{align*}

Now we want to find the area of the circle/sector.
\begin{align*}
\text{circle/sector area } = \frac{\theta}{2 \pi} \text{ pie area } = \frac{\theta}{2 \pi} \left( 2 \pi r \right) = \theta r
\end{align*}
Substitute $\theta$ with twice the angle between the $y$-axis and the line segment $AB$. Using the Law of Cosines,
\begin{align*}
d^2 &= 81 = (10-2)^2 + r^2 + 2 \cdot (10-2)r \cdot \cos \theta \\
\therefore \ \cos \theta &= \frac{d^2 - (10-2)^2 - r^2}{2 \cdot (10-2)r} = \frac{81 - 81 - 20.25}{2 \cdot 8 \cdot 4.5} = -0.107 \\
\therefore \ \theta &= \arccos(-0.107)\, \text{ radians}
\end{align*}

To find the angle in degrees instead of radians, multiply by $180/\pi$:

\begin{align*}
\theta \approx 6.02\, \text{ degrees }
\end{align*}

