\begin{align*}
\cos^3 x + \cos^2 x &= \frac{1}{2} (1 + 2\cos x + 2\cos x(1- 4\sin^2 x + 3) + 1 + 1 - 4\sin^2 x + 3) \\
&= \frac{1}{2} (1 + 2\cos x - 8\cos x \sin^2 x + 7 + 2 - 4\sin^2 x + 3) \\
\end{align*}

Combine the constants and simplify:

\begin{align*}
\cos^3 x + \cos^2 x &= \frac{1}{2} (9+ 2 - 8\cos x \sin^2 x) \\
&= 4.5 - 4\cos x \sin^2 x \\
&= \boxed{ 4.5 - 4\cos x \left(1 - \cos^2 x \right)}
\end{align*}

