We are given two binomial coefficients: $\binom{14}{8} = 3003$ and $\binom{14}{7} = 3432$.

From the combinatorics perspective, $\binom{n}{r}=\frac{n!}{r!(n-r)!}$, so $\binom{n+1}{r}=\binom{n}{r}+\binom{n}{r-1}$. Let $x=\binom{15}{7}$. Then:
\begin{align*}
x &= \binom{15}{7}\\
&= \binom{14+1}{7} = \binom{14}{7}+\binom{14}{6}\\
&= 3432+3003 = \boxed{6435}
\end{align*}

Note: In general, it is better to use the formula for binomial coefficients and calculate it step by step when the number is relatively large. In situations where you are provided with a few neighboring binomial coefficients, you can use the recursive formula (which requires less operations) to avoid unnecessary calculations.

